Mood or Vibe: Civil disobedience executed with the tenderness of a hand-knitted blanket; defiant, rain-dampened infrastructure transformed into a womb of amber-lit peace.

Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Clocks Bleed Across the Pelican Walk
- Story & Context: At 7:00 AM under the Blackfriars Flyover, Nadia Vane slid copper alligator clips into the intersection’s timing module, locking all four approaches into an unmoving amber halt while her viola droned against the groaning chassis of three hundred idling vehicles. Exhaust fumes hung in the cold morning drizzle like incense as she leaned into a heavy, tape-saturated ostinato, drawing commuters out of their cabs not with fury, but with the scent of fried roadside transformer oil and the soothing pulse of her bassline. When the municipal tow-trucks arrived, their drivers refused to hook the chains, quietly cutting their engines just as Nadia murmured through a repurposed megaphone, "Take your foot off the pedal, love, the morning is cancelled."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A split-screen painted album cover aesthetic, on one side showing a rain-smeared intersection jammed with delivery trucks, on the other a close-up of a wooden viola bridge entangled with colorful electrical jumper wires, with the lyric "Take your foot off the pedal, love, the morning is cancelled" stamped like a red parking citation across the bottom.

Track 2: "We Cancelled the Five O’Clock Meeting for This?"
- Story & Context: Crammed inside an electrical relay shelter at Holbeck Junction, Levon cradled his bass guitar against his ribs and admitted to the circle of freezing protesters that he hadn't joined the sabotage to save the public docks, but because the incessant ping of company calendar alerts had hollowed out his skull. He fed his signal through a hacked pedestrian audio-locator chirp, muting its shrill city-council warning into a pillow-soft, rhythmic thud that had three corporate couriers sitting on the cold curb sharing his tin flask of chicory brew. Rain tapped against the sheet-metal roof in steady syncopation with his unhurried fingers as Levon leaned his forehead against the mic stand to exhale, "My spine is just a punch-card counting down to nothing."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A moody, cinematic still of a corporate digital wristwatch submerged in a puddle on wet macadam, its screen displaying an unanswered calendar alert, softly reflecting the amber bulb of a pedestrian signal box draped in hand-spun wool yarn.

Track 3: Signal-to-Noise on an Abandoned Sunday
- Story & Context: Long after the riot squads cleared the blockades and winter turned to rot, the electrical relays at Junction 4 retained a permanent, magnetic ghost of Tariq’s tape-delay loops embedded deep within their copper coils. Every Sunday at dusk, the automated highway gantries would mysteriously bypass city servers, displaying cryptic calendar coordinates while the bridge's expansion joints vibrated with a deep, resonant D-minor viola hum. Long-haul truck drivers began detouring hundreds of miles just to idle beneath the empty concrete culvert, turning off their radios to listen to the pavement softly whisper, "No one clocks in when the tarmac learns to sing."
